    "narrative": "The engine room for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is led by **Corbin Carroll**, who boasts the day\u2019s premier score of 78.75. Facing Lodolo, **Corbin Carroll** capitalizes on a high-upside matchup against a lefty with a vulnerable 4.68 xERA; the combination of the favorable park factor and his elite speed profile\u2014critical for our SB-starved squad\u2014solidifies his status as our must-start anchor. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (67.49) and **Isaac Paredes** (65.97) follow closely, both benefiting from dynamic platoon advantages that inflate their raw projections. **Gabriel Moreno** (65.11) also earns a strong recommendation, as his #3 spot in the order against a weak arm in Lodolo presents an optimal environment for production.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Griffin Conine**, who sits on the bench despite an impressive xwOBA of 0.396. While **Griffin Conine** held a strong platoon edge, the optimizer ultimately pivoted to **Josh Naylor** (58.40) and **Henry Bolte** (56.01) due to their better baseline consistency and more favorable lineup spots. We also opted to keep **Brice Turang** on the bench; while **Brice Turang** is a constant threat in the SB category, the matchup against an elite arm like Sale (2.83 xERA) effectively nuked his efficiency score, pushing him below our bench threshold.\n\nManagers should proceed with caution regarding the lower end of our lineup. **Cole Young** (46.26) and **Max Muncy** (48.86) remain in the lineup due to the scarcity of superior alternatives, but their sub-50 scores underscore the volatility of these spots today. Specifically, the matchup for **Mickey Moniak** (53.27) is complicated by an elite opposing catcher whose 1.86s pop time acts as a drag on his SB upside, even with a favorable park factor. Keep a close watch on late-breaking weather reports, as the margin for error is razor-thin for the back half of our starters.",
